    <statement><p>Rewrite the following expression into the form of a+b<m>i</m>:</p>
<p><m>\displaystyle{ \frac{{5-6i}}{{-6-5i}} = }</m><fillin characters="11" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>i</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p>By definition, <m>i=\sqrt{-1}</m>, so we have <m>i^{2}=-1</m>.</p>
<p>To get rid of <alert><m>i</m></alert> in the denominator, we use the difference of squares formula:</p>
<p><m>\displaystyle{ 
 \begin{aligned}
   \amp \phantom{{}=}(-6+{-5i})(-6-{-5i}) \\
   \amp = (-6)^{2}-({-5i})^{2} \\
   \amp = (36)-(-25) \\
   \amp = 61
 \end{aligned}
    }</m></p>
<p>The full solution is:</p>
<p><m>\displaystyle{ 
\begin{aligned}
   \amp \phantom{{}=} \frac{{5-6i}}{{-6-5i}} \\
   \amp = \frac{({5-6i})(-6-{-5i})}{({-6-5i})(-6-{-5i})}  \\
   \amp = \frac{(5)(-6)+(5)(-{-5i})+({-6i})(-6)+({-6i})(-{-5i})}{61}  \\
   \amp = \frac{(-30)+(25i)+(36i)+(-30)(i^{2})}{61}  \\
   \amp = \frac{(-30)+(61i)+(-30)(-1)}{61}  \\
   \amp = \frac{(-30)+(61i)+(30)}{61}  \\
   \amp = \frac{(0)+(61i)}{61}  \\
   \amp = \frac{0}{61}+\frac{61i}{61}  \\
   \amp = {i}
\end{aligned}
    }</m></p>
</solution>
